================================================================================
         CLAUDE CODE — FROM INSTALLATION TO BUILDING THE METAR APP
================================================================================

This guide covers everything from installing Claude Code to how we built the
full METAR weather application step by step.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT IS CLAUDE CODE?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Claude Code is Anthropic's official AI-powered command-line tool (CLI) that
lets you interact with Claude directly inside your terminal or IDE. It can:

  - Read, write, and edit files in your project
  - Run shell commands and terminal scripts
  - Search and navigate codebases
  - Fix bugs, add features, and explain code
  - Commit to git and push to GitHub
  - Spawn background agents for parallel tasks

It is NOT a chatbot tab — it lives inside your terminal and operates directly
on your real files and system.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. REQUIREMENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - Node.js version 18 or higher
  - npm (comes with Node.js)
  - A terminal (Linux, macOS, or Windows WSL)
  - An Anthropic API key  (get one at console.anthropic.com)
  - Git (optional, for version control tasks)

Check your Node version:
  node --version

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. INSTALLATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1 — Install Claude Code globally via npm:

  npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code

Step 2 — Verify the installation:

  claude --version

Step 3 — Authenticate with your Anthropic API key:

  claude

  On first run it will open a browser window to log in or ask for your API key.
  Your key is stored securely and used for all future sessions.

  Alternatively, set it as an environment variable:

    export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

  Add that line to your ~/.bashrc or ~/.zshrc to persist it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. STARTING A SESSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Navigate to your project folder and start Claude Code:

  cd /your/project
  claude

This opens an interactive prompt. You can type instructions in plain English.
Claude reads your files, writes code, and runs commands — all with your
approval.

Useful startup flags:

  claude                      # Start interactive session
  claude "fix the login bug"  # One-shot command, then exit
  claude --help               # See all available flags

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. KEY COMMANDS INSIDE CLAUDE CODE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  /help          — Show all available slash commands
  /clear         — Clear the conversation context
  /config        — Change settings (model, theme, etc.)
  /compact       — Compress conversation to save context
  /cost          — Show token usage and cost for this session
  /exit or /quit — End the session

Keyboard shortcuts:

  Enter          — Send your message
  Shift+Enter    — Add a new line without sending
  Ctrl+C         — Cancel the current tool call or interrupt
  Up/Down arrow  — Browse previous messages

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. HOW CLAUDE CODE WORKS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

When you type an instruction, Claude:

  1. Reads relevant files in your project
  2. Reasons about what needs to change
  3. Proposes a tool call (edit file / run command / search)
  4. Waits for your approval (or auto-approves if you allow it)
  5. Executes the action and shows you the result
  6. Continues until the task is complete

You stay in control — every file edit and shell command is shown before it runs.

Permission modes:

  Default  — Claude asks permission for most actions
  Auto     — Claude runs approved tool types without asking each time
  Manual   — Claude asks before every single action
